      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "The early 1990s marked a golden age for Hong Kong action cinema, with directors pushing technical and narrative boundaries. <a href=\"/movie/11782\" class=\"movie-title\">Hard Boiled</a> (1992) and Full Contact released the same year, showcasing different approaches to action filmmaking - Woo's balletic violence versus Lam's brutal realism. These films directly influenced Hollywood, with directors like Quentin Tarantino (<a href=\"/movie/500\" class=\"movie-title\" data-tmdb-id=\"500\">Reservoir Dogs</a> (1992)) and Robert Rodriguez (<a href=\"/movie/8068\" class=\"movie-title\" data-tmdb-id=\"8068\">Desperado</a> (1995)) incorporating their stylistic elements."
      },
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "Ringo Lam's approach to action choreography emphasized visceral impact over aesthetic grace. This influenced later films like The Raid (2011) and <a href=\"/movie/245891\" class=\"movie-title\" data-tmdb-id=\"245891\">John Wick</a> (2014), which similarly prioritized brutal efficiency in their action sequences. The film's themes of betrayal and redemption through violence became staples of the genre, echoing through works like Kill Bill Vol. 1 (2003) and <a href=\"/movie/64690\" class=\"movie-title\" data-tmdb-id=\"64690\">Drive</a> (2011). SUBHEAD: Impact on Modern Action Cinema"
      },
